Your cluster has no HPA or VPA configured — and your inflate deployment is the classic Karpenter over-provisioning pattern (a pause container with inflated resource requests to pre-warm nodes). Here is the complete guide:

Right-sizing Pods with Karpenter Over-provisioning
Why Karpenter over-provisions — and what it means
Your inflate deployment uses the over-provisioning pattern: it runs pause containers with large resource requests to hold capacity on nodes. When a real workload arrives, it evicts the pause pods and the node is already warm — no cold-start delay.

The problem is the requests are guesses. If they are too large, Karpenter provisions bigger/more nodes than needed. The fix is a two-part approach:

1. VPA  → right-size individual pod CPU/memory requests automatically
2. HPA  → scale pod count horizontally based on actual usage
3. Karpenter Disruption Budget → control how aggressively Karpenter consolidates

Part 1 — Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA)
VPA watches actual CPU/memory usage and automatically adjusts pod requests to match real consumption. This directly fixes Karpenter over-provisioning because Karpenter sizes nodes based on pod requests.

Install VPA
# Clone the VPA repo
git clone https://github.com/kubernetes/autoscaler.git
cd autoscaler/vertical-pod-autoscaler

# Install VPA components (recommender, updater, admission controller)
./hack/vpa-up.sh

# Verify all 3 VPA components are running
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep vpa
# Expected:
# vpa-admission-controller-xxx   Running
# vpa-recommender-xxx            Running
# vpa-updater-xxx                Running

Run in CloudShell
Apply VPA to your inflate deployment
# vpa-inflate.yaml
apiVersion: autoscaling.k8s.io/v1
kind: VerticalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: inflate-vpa
  namespace: default
spec:
  targetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: inflate
  updatePolicy:
    updateMode: "Auto"       # Auto = VPA restarts pods with new requests
                             # "Off" = recommendation only, no changes
                             # "Initial" = only on new pods, no restarts
  resourcePolicy:
    containerPolicies:
    - containerName: "*"
      minAllowed:
        cpu: 50m
        memory: 64Mi
      maxAllowed:
        cpu: 4
        memory: 8Gi
      controlledResources: ["cpu", "memory"]

kubectl apply -f vpa-inflate.yaml

# Check VPA recommendations after ~10 minutes of traffic
kubectl describe vpa inflate-vpa -n default
# Look for:
#   Lower Bound:   cpu: 25m    memory: 262144k
#   Target:        cpu: 100m   memory: 512Mi    ← VPA will set requests to this
#   Upper Bound:   cpu: 500m   memory: 2Gi

Run in CloudShell
⚠️ Do not run VPA Auto mode together with HPA on the same metric (CPU/memory). They conflict. Use VPA for requests sizing + HPA on custom metrics (RPS, queue depth), or use VPA in Off mode to get recommendations and apply them manually.

Part 2 — Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)
HPA scales the number of pod replicas based on CPU, memory, or custom metrics. This works alongside Karpenter — more pods → Karpenter adds nodes; fewer pods → Karpenter consolidates nodes.

Prerequisite — install metrics-server
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server/releases/latest/download/components.yaml

# Verify
kubectl top nodes
kubectl top pods -A

Run in CloudShell
Apply HPA to inflate deployment
# hpa-inflate.yaml
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: inflate-hpa
  namespace: default
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: inflate
  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 20
  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 60    # scale up when avg CPU > 60%
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: memory
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 70    # scale up when avg memory > 70%
  behavior:
    scaleUp:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 60     # wait 60s before scaling up
      policies:
      - type: Pods
        value: 2
        periodSeconds: 60               # add max 2 pods per minute
    scaleDown:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300   # wait 5 min before scaling down
      policies:
      - type: Percent
        value: 25
        periodSeconds: 120              # remove max 25% of pods per 2 min

kubectl apply -f hpa-inflate.yaml

# Watch HPA in action
kubectl get hpa -n default -w
# NAME           REFERENCE             TARGETS         MINPODS   MAXPODS   REPLICAS
# inflate-hpa    Deployment/inflate    12%/60%         2         20        2

