Part 3 — Fix Karpenter over-provisioning directly
The inflate deployment itself is the over-provisioning mechanism. Right-size its requests so Karpenter doesn't hold excess capacity:

Check current inflate requests
kubectl get deployment inflate -n default -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[*].resources}'

Run in CloudShell
Tune the inflate deployment requests
# Right-sized inflate deployment — match to your actual workload headroom need
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: inflate
  namespace: default
spec:
  replicas: 1                    # reduce replicas — 1 is usually enough for warm capacity
  template:
    spec:
      priorityClassName: low-priority   # CRITICAL — real pods evict inflate pods
      containers:
      - name: pause
        image: public.ecr.aws/eks-distro/kubernetes/pause:3.7
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "1"             # tune this to match ONE real pod's expected size
            memory: "1Gi"        # tune this to match ONE real pod's expected size

Create a low priority class for inflate pods
# priority-class.yaml — inflate pods are evicted first when real workloads arrive
apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: low-priority
value: -1                        # negative = below default (0)
globalDefault: false
description: "Used by inflate over-provisioning pods — evicted first"

kubectl apply -f priority-class.yaml

