Part 4 — Karpenter consolidation (Auto Mode)
Since your cluster uses EKS Auto Mode (Karpenter-based), configure disruption settings to consolidate over-provisioned nodes automatically:

# nodepool-consolidation.yaml — tune the general-purpose node pool
apiVersion: karpenter.sh/v1
kind: NodePool
metadata:
  name: general-purpose
spec:
  disruption:
    consolidationPolicy: WhenEmptyOrUnderutilized   # consolidate underutilized nodes
    consolidateAfter: 30s                            # wait 30s after pod eviction
    budgets:
    - nodes: "20%"                                   # never disrupt more than 20% of nodes at once
  template:
    spec:
      expireAfter: 720h                              # recycle nodes every 30 days

kubectl apply -f nodepool-consolidation.yaml

# Watch Karpenter consolidation decisions
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l app.kubernetes.io/name=karpenter -f | grep -i consolidat

Run in CloudShell
Decision guide — which tool to use
Is your pod using too much CPU/memory per replica?
  └─ YES → Use VPA (right-size requests)
           kubectl describe vpa <name> to see recommendations

Are you getting too many/few pod replicas under load?
  └─ YES → Use HPA (scale replicas based on utilization)
           kubectl get hpa -A to check current state

Are nodes too large for the workloads on them?
  └─ YES → Tune inflate deployment requests downward
           + enable Karpenter consolidation policy

Are nodes staying alive with very low utilization?
  └─ YES → Set consolidationPolicy: WhenEmptyOrUnderutilized on NodePool
           + reduce consolidateAfter to 30s

